---
import Figure from "@/components/Figure.astro";
import safariExtensionImage from "@/assets/safari_extension.jpg";

type Link = {
  label: string;
  url?: string;
}

const books = [
  {
    title: "White Nights",
    author: "Fyodor Dostoevsky",
    cover: whiteNightsImage,
    url: "https://objectiveink.gumroad.com/l/whitenights",
    alt: "Cover and interior spread of my edition of White Nights",
  },
  {
    title: "The Sorrows of Young Werther",
    author: "Johann Wolfgang von Goethe",
    cover: wertherImage,
    url: "https://objectiveink.gumroad.com/l/werther",
    alt: "Cover and interior spread of my edition of The Sorrows of Young Werther",
  },
];

import wertherImage from "@/assets/werther.jpg";
import whiteNightsImage from "@/assets/whitenights.jpg";

---

<section id="design" class="page-section" aria-labelledby="design-h">
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="col-8">
      <h2 id="design-h">2—Design</h2>

      <p>
        How can I build good things if I only know how to code? I started improving myself as a
          designer so I could do things on my own, from conceptualization to release, and make sure
          they look good.
      </p>
      <p>
        With enough experience, I realized that code must represent structurally what design is
          semantically. The unification of code and design opens a different level of thinking that
          can be defined as systems thinking, and with enough work and effort, it can be applied as a
          broad skill set to product design.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="page-subsection" aria-labelledby="minimalist-h">
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="col-6">
        <h3 id="minimalist-h">2.1—Minimalist Design</h3>
        <p>
          An experimental Safari extension written in Swift and JavaScript that modifies the design
            of websites to fit my taste: typography-oriented, no ornamentation, only what’s necessary
            to make the content the main subject and as legible as possible.
        </p>
        <p>Currently on hold, but I’ll upgrade it soon and port it to Chrome too.</p>
        <p>
          <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/minimalist-design/id6765779070?mt=12">See on the App Store</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="col-6">
        <Figure
          src={safariExtensionImage}
          alt="The CTAN page for the microtype package, restyled by the Minimalist Design extension"
          ratio="8 / 5"
          class="offset"
        >
          See the <a href="https://ctan.org/pkg/microtype" target="_blank">original page</a> for the example shown in this picture.
        </Figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-subsection" aria-labelledby="typography-h">

    <div class="grid">
      <div class="col-6">
        <h3 id="typography-h">2.2—Typography</h3>
        <p>
          I’ve taken public domain books and designed my own editions to make them more enjoyable to
            read and give them the typographical treatment they deserve, making the design reflect the
            tone of the stories. Both the interiors and the covers are my design. I plan to release more
            books eventually.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <ul class="books grid">
      {
            books.map((b) => (
              <li class="book col-6">
                <Figure src={b.cover} alt={b.alt} ratio="16 / 9" />
                <h4><a href={b.url}>{b.title}</a></h4>
                <p class="author">{b.author}</p>
                <p class="purchase"><a href={b.url}>Purchase on Gumroad</a></p>
              </li>
            ))
          }
    </ul>
  </section>
</section>

<style>
.books {
  list-style: none;
}

.book {
  a {
    text-decoration: none;
  }
}

.author {
  font-size: var(--text-size-5);
  letter-spacing: 0.02em;
}

h4 {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
}

.purchase {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;

  a {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }
}
</style>
